WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.728
ROBERT KUBIS: Have you ever
set up or managed a distributed

00:00:02.728 --> 00:00:05.070
database and are tired of
the operational headache?

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.100
Or, are you considering moving
to one for scalability and high

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.750
availability reasons?

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.390
Oftentimes, you have to
choose between scalability

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.500
and consistency.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.750
But also, ease of
setup and manageability

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.850
are important
factors to consider.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.500
Hi, I'm Robert Kubis.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.180
And in this video
series, we talk

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.240
about Google Cloud Spanner,
a fully managed relational

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.990
database service.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.810
So do you want to
know how to provision

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.210
and scale a mission
critical database in seconds

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.970
without having to worry about
consistency or maintenance

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:33.870
windows?

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.190
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.594
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:40.010
When you hear about
Cloud Spanner,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.600
many things feel and
sound like magic.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.360
A database that is horizontally
scalable while offering

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.530
strong consistency across zones,
regions, or even globally--

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.940
how is this possible?

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.940
Before we look into some of
the details and technologies

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.180
how Cloud Spanner works, let
us get your own instance up

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.510
and running first.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.740
In the Google Cloud
Console, search for Spanner.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.750
Click on Create
Instance, and you'll

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.290
find all you need to set up
your instance in one screen.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.910
You just name the instance.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.000
Select the configuration
and how many nodes you want.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:12.510
Done.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.100
That was easy, right?

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.340
But wait.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.530
What just happened?

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.030
Let's take a moment
here and talk

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.130
about which
configuration to choose

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.970
and how many nodes to select.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.390
In Cloud Spanner,
compute nodes and storage

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.050
are separated to enable
scaling each independently.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.540
On top, new nodes and
data are replicated

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.030
across multiple zones,
or even regions,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.560
for higher availability.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.210
When choosing a
configuration, you

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.460
have the option between
a regional instance

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.520
in a particular location
or a multi-region instance.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.550
Let's drill down a
bit further there.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.070
In a regional configuration,
your instance, compute,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.440
and storage gets
distributed and replicated

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.570
across three zones
within one region--

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.930
for instance, your rest
or your essential one--

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.960
making sure your data stays
available and consistent

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.660
across hardware maintenance,
software patches,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.110
or even failure
of an entire zone.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.150
But what if you need
higher availability

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.250
guarantees and a
better read performance

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.229
from different places?

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.760
In that case, you
can choose one of

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.920
our multi-regional
configurations, which

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.680
distributes your instance
to up to four regions

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.220
across the globe.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.110
This allows for an entire
region to become available,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.870
while your database still
works as normal from all

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.430
the other regions.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.340
Having your data available
in more than one region also

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.100
enables faster reads
from those regions.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.340
And the best of all?

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.270
You don't have to worry about
setting up your own replication

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.100
or failure instances.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.470
All this is fully managed
for you in one setting.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.840
Now, what about nodes?

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.100
For each node you request
for your instance,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.880
you get compute resources
in all zones and regions,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.070
depending on your
selected configuration.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.100
There's no need for difficult
planning, and forecasting,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.680
and how many resources
to provision upfront.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.410
You can just change the number
of instance nodes any time

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.130
you need more or fewer.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:03.240
You just click on Edit Instance,
change the number of nodes,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:03.960
and done.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.530
Really easy.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.130
And if you like your keyboard
more than your pointing device,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.000
you can do all this
from the command line

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.150
as well, or with
our client libraries

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.240
that are available in
many, many languages.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.220
Your instance is
now up and running.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.470
Actually, it has been
ready and waiting for us

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.690
since we clicked that
little Create button.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.720
So let's go ahead and create
a database and schema.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.760
It's recommended to do
both at the same time.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.810
Schemas in Cloud
Spanner are just

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.120
like any other
relational database.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.060
You have tables and columns
that are strongly typed.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.190
And if you need to
add a table, or index,

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.290
or remove a column
in the future,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.665
you can just do so without
any downtime incurred.

00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:45.810
Cloud spanner runs
schema changes

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.060
in a long-running transaction.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.090
But keep in mind, depending
on individual statements

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.490
that you execute
for schema changes,

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.500
they can range from
minutes for adding

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.660
a column to hours
for backfilling

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.270
an index on a large database.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.140
Now that your
database is created,

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.820
you can use our client libraries
to write and read data.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.950
Cloud Spanner also
speaks SQL, which

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.200
lets you reuse and
test your existing

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.990
skills of working with
traditional relational

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.250
databases.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.800
The next step is to look
into loading some data

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.079
and how to do this efficiently.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.959
Unfortunately, we are out
of time in this episode,

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.190
and we will keep that
for the next one.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.260
So if you want to learn
more about Cloud Spanner

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.290
best practices and
inner workings,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.120
follow this featured
series and check out

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.000
the links in the description.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.650
In the next episode of
"Things-I-Learned about Cloud

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.100
Spanner," we will look at how
to pick the right primary keys

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.880
to enable fast and
efficient writes and reads.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.910
And remember, you don't need
to sacrifice consistency

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:45.640
for scalability.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

